"# Remedial — Limitation vs Description\n\n**Time:** ~1 hour | **Artifact:** reflection.md\n\nYou described the sample's skew. You did not state its limitation. These are different acts.\n\n## Context\nA description says what the data looks like. A limitation says what the data cannot support because of what it looks like. Graders (and stakeholders) only trust the second.\n\n## Do\n1. Take one skewed variable from your data audit and write its description in one sentence\n2. Rewrite it as a generalization boundary: name the population your finding does NOT extend to\n3. Repeat for two more variables\n\n## Reflection\nFor each pair, explain why the boundary version changes what a stakeholder is allowed to do with the finding.\n\n## What the grader checks\nYour reflection must show the misconception is gone: boundaries phrased as implications, not restatements of skew."
